# Untitled

Multi-modal anti-counterfeiting and encryption enabled through silicon-based materials featuring pH-responsive fluorescence and room-temperature phosphorescence
Jinhua Wang§, Bin Song§, Jiali Tang, Guyue Hu, Jingyang Wang, Mingyue Cui, and Yao He (✉)
Laboratory of Nanoscale Biochemical Analysis, Institute of Functional Nano and Soft Materials (FUNSOM), and Collaborative Innovation Center of Suzhou Nano Science and Technology, Soochow University, Suzhou 215123, China § Jinhua Wang and Bin Song contributed equally to this work.
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2020 Received: 22 December 2019 / Revised: 28 March 2020 / Accepted: 28 March 2020
## ABSTRACT
Optical silicon (Si)-based materials are highly attractive due to their widespread applications ranging from electronics to biomedicine. It is worth noting that while extensive efforts have been devoted to developing fluorescent Si-based structures, there currently exist no examples of Si-based materials featuring phosphorescence emission, severely limiting Si-based wide-ranging optical applications. To address this critical issue, we herein introduce a kind of Si-based material, in which metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are in-situ growing on the surface of Si nanoparticles (SiNPs) assisted by microwave irradiation. Of particular significance, the resultant materials, i.e., MOFs-encapsulated SiNPs (MOFs@SiNPs) could exhibit pH-responsive fluorescence, whose maximum emission wavelength is red-shifted from 442 to 592 nm when the pH increases from 2 to 13. More importantly, distinct room-temperature phosphorescence (maximum emission wavelength, 505 nm) could be observed in this system, with long lifetime of 215 ms. Taking advantages of above-mentioned unique optical properties, the MOFs@SiNPs are further employed as high-quality anti-counterfeiting inks for advanced encryption. In comparison to conventional fluorescence anti-counterfeiting techniques (static fluorescence outputs are generally used, thus being easily duplicated and leading to counterfeiting risk), pH-responsive fluorescence and room-temperature phosphorescence of the resultant MOFs@SiNPs-based ink could offer advanced multi-modal security, which is therefore capable of realizing higher-level information security against counterfeiting.
## KEYWORDS
silicon nanohybrids, pH-responsive fluorescence, room-temperature phosphorescence, multi-modal anti-counterfeiting
## 1 Introduction
Optical silicon (Si)-based materials have drawn extensive attentions since the last century. With the significant advancement of nanotechnology, fluorescent zero-dimensional Si nanoparticles (SiNPs) have been successfully prepared since they could produce visible fluorescence when their sizes reach nanoscale level (generally smaller than 5 nm). In recent years, elegant effort has been devoted for the fabrication of high-quality SiNPs featuring strong fluorescence and robust photostability [1–6]. Moreover, small-sized (diameter < 10 nm) SiNPs have recently entered into the first clinic trial due to their excellent biodegradability and negligible toxicity [7, 8]. It is worth pointing out that, while tremendous progresses have been achieved for the production of fluorescent Si-based materials, there currently exist scanty examples of Si-based materials simultaneously featuring fluorescence and phosphorescence properties, which is adverse to promoting Si-based widespread optical applications.On the other hand, as a global problem, counterfeiting causes severe economic and security impacts on the government, industry, and consumers [9, 10]. Therefore, the development of anti-counterfeiting system with reliable security has triggered extensive research. Over the last decade, a number
