In this work, we assemble the SiNPs with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs, served as hosts) under microwave irradiation, producing the MOFs-encapsulated SiNPs (MOFs@SiNPs). Significantly, the resultant MOFs@SiNPs display pH-dependent fluorescence, whose maximum emission wavelength increases from \(\sim442\,\mathrm{nm}\) (pH value: 2) to \(\sim592\,\mathrm{nm}\) (pH value: 13). Besides, the MOFs@SiNPs exhibit strong phosphorescence (maximum emission wavelength: \(\sim505\;\mathrm{nm}\) ) under room temperature with
long lifetime (more than 5 s to naked eye). Taking advantages of the unique optical features, the MOFs@SiNPs are further exploited as high-performance optical inks, superbly suitable for the multi-modal encryption and fingerprint anti-counterfeiting.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis of MOFs@SiNPs
Preparation of the MOFs@SiNPs and their luminescent properties are schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. Specifically, the SiNPs can be readily encapsulated by metal–organic frameworks in the self-assembly process through microwave reaction. In our system, there is affinity between SiNPs surfaces and carboxylate groups of organic linker molecules, facilitating the adsorption of the terephthalic acid on the SiNPs surface. Therefore, the zinc carboxylate secondary building units (SBUs) can be formed on the surface of SiNPs (Step 1) [30, 31]. The paddle-wheel dinuclear zinc carboxylate SBUs are bridged by the terephthalic acid (TPA) ligands to form two-dimensional layers. These layers are stacked in an offset fashion, which would produce the three-dimensional frameworks embedded with SiNPs, therefore leading to the formation of the hybrid MOFs@SiNPs (Step 2) [32–35]. Of
particular significance, under ultraviolet (UV) irradiation, the resultant MOFs@SiNPs exhibit blue fluorescence emission at \(442~\mathrm{nm}\) (arising from the encapsulated SiNPs). Moreover, the MOFs@SiNPs show green phosphorescence emission (maximum emission wavelength: \(\sim505\,\mathrm{nm}\)) at room temperature after turning off the UV lamp, which can be attributed to the metal-ligand charge transfer in metal-organic frameworks [35]. In addition, due to the structural transformation of the MOFs@SiNPs in alkaline condition \((\mathrm{pH}=13)\), the fluorescence emission can be tuned from blue to orange (with emission wavelength of \(592\;\mathrm{nm}\)).
## 2.2 Characterizations of MOFs@SiNPs
in MOF matrix. The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (inset in Fig. 2(c)) shows that the distinct crystal lattice spacing \(\sim 0.19\,\mathrm{nm}\), further confirming the crystallization of the encapsulated SiNPs [36]. Furthermore, the resultant MOFs@SiNPs are characterized by energy dispersive X-ray spectra (EDX, Fig. 2(d)), X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS, Fig. 2(e), Fig. S3 in the ESM), X-ray powder diffraction (XRD, Fig. 2(f)), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR, Fig. 2(g)), and thermal gravity analysis (TGA, Fig. S4 in the ESM). In addition, the high-resolution XPS C 1s spectra of MOFs and SiNPs are supplied in Fig. S5 in the ESM. Typically, the EDX mapping in Fig. 2(d) suggests that apart from common elements (e.g., Zn, C, and O) of pure MOFs, Si element is detected in the resultant MOFs@SiNPs. For the XPS spectra, distinct peaks are observed at around \(103\,\mathrm{eV}\) (Si 2p), \(286\,\mathrm{eV}\) (C 1s), \(533\,\mathrm{eV}\) (O 1s) and \(1,022\,\mathrm{eV}\) (Zn 2p) (Fig. 2(e)), demonstrating the presence of Si, C, O and Zn elements in the MOFs@SiNPs. XRD pattern of the MOFs-encapsulated SiNPs is similar to that of the MOFs (Fig. 2(f)), indicating that the combination with SiNPs does not destroy the structure of the MOFs. The XRD spectrum of SiNPs shows broad reflection arising from the silicon oxides. In comparison to the strong peaks associated with MOFs, the peaks of the SiNPs are not obviously found in MOFs@SiNPs, which might be due to their low concentrations and/or small sizes [37–39]. The FTIR spectra (Fig. 2(g)) show that the prepared MOFs@SiNPs have many characteristic bands associated with MOFs and SiNPs. The asymmetric stretching of carboxylate group in MOFs appears in around \(1,588\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [40, 41]. Several bands in the region of \(1,200{-}700\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be attributed to the out-of-plane vibration of terephthalic acid [41]. The signal at \(1,010\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for Si–O bond confirms the presence of silicon atoms. Compared with parent MOFs, the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of MOFs@SiNPs is decreased (Fig. 3e in the ESM), which implies that the SiNPs are successfully encapsulated in the MOFs matrix.
The optical properties of the MOFs@SiNPs are characterized (Fig. 3, Figs. S7 and S8 in the ESM). The obtained MOFs@SiNPs display blue fluorescence (quantum yield (QY): \(\sim17\%\), Fig. S9 in the ESM) under UV irradiation \((365\,\mathrm{nm})\), and interestingly, green phosphorescence can be observed by the naked eye after turning off the UV light. In this system, the zinc ions contribute to the frontier orbital distribution, which is able to promote spin-orbit coupling. On the other hand, the rigid framework may result in restriction of the molecular vibrations and nonradiative loss, which increases singlet-to-triplet intersystem crossing (ISC) and thus leads to the production of green phosphorescence [42–44]. Moreover, the phosphorescence duration could be recognized for approximately \(5\,\mathrm{s}\) by a digital camera (Fig. 3(a)). When dispersed in NaOH solution \(\mathrm{pH}=13\), the fluorescence emission of the MOFs@SiNPs shows a remarkable transformation from a blue to orange colour, while the phosphorescence disappears (Fig. 3(b)). The fluorescence spectrum of MOFs@SiNPs presents the maximum emission peak at \(442\,\mathrm{nm}\) produced by the encapsulated SiNPs (Fig. 3(c)), suggesting the formation of host–guest system between MOFs and SiNPs. As the \(\mathrm{pH}\) value increases from 2 to 13, the fluorescence emission shows a red shift from 442 to \(592\,\mathrm{nm}\) with excitation wavelength at \(365\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figs. S10(a) and S10(b) in the ESM). For the MOFs@SiNPs, the structural transformation of the Zn-based SBUs occurs due to the hydrolysis of the coordinated N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) molecules in the MOFs@SiNPs channels [32, 45, 46], which would induce the reduced band gap, therefore leading to the emission wavelength red-shift [35, 47]. The XRD results further show that the MOFs@SiNPs present an obvious structural change after
treatment with \(\mathrm{pH}\,12\) and \(\mathrm{pH}\,13\) basic solution, which are in agreement with TEM characterizations (Figs. S10(c) and S11 in the ESM). In addition to the fluorescence emission under UV excitation, the MOFs@SiNPs composites exhibit distinct green phosphorescence at about \(505\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 3(d)). As shown in Fig. 3(e), phosphorescence decay curves of the MOFs and MOFs@SiNPs can be fitted with a single exponential function. According to the following equation: \(I(t)=\mathrm{Ae}^{-1/\tau}\), phosphorescence lifetimes of the MOFs and MOFs@SiNPs are \(226\,\mathrm{ms}\) (half-life: \(174.10\,\mathrm{ms}\)) and \(215\,\mathrm{ms}\) (half-life: \(171.30\,\mathrm{ms}\)), respectively. For deeper understanding the room-temperature phosphorescence property of the MOFs@SiNPs, the low temperature \((77\,\mathrm{K})\) fluorescence and phosphorescence spectra of the MOFs@SiNPs are measured (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Accordingly, the energy gap between the lowest triplet state \((T_{1})\) and the lowest singlet state \((S_{1})\) is calculated to be \(0.33\,\mathrm{eV}\) \((T_{1}=2.53\,\mathrm{eV},\,S_{1}=2.86\,\mathrm{eV})\), and such a small value is favourable for an effective ISC process. Furthermore, we investigate the photostability of the MOFs@SiNPs. As shown in Fig. S13 in the ESM, the MOFs@SiNPs maintain stable fluorescence (preserving \(\sim84\%\) of the initial intensity) and phosphorescence (preserving \(\sim73\%\) of the initial intensity) under continuous UV exposure for \(200\,\mathrm{min}\). The assays of cellular viability (Henrietta Lacks (HeLa) cells and African green monkey kidney cells (COS-7) treated by the MOFs@SiNPs preserve over \(85\%\) at different concentrations and incubation time (12 and \(24\,\mathrm{h}\))) demonstrate the negligible cytotoxicity of the resultant MOFs@SiNPs (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Such attractive optical properties and favorable biocompatibility of Si enable the prepared MOFs@SiNPs an advanced security ink for multi-responsive anti-counterfeiting applications, as demonstrated in the following experiments.
## 2.3 Multi-modal anti-counterfeiting
We further exploit the MOFs@SiNPs as novel high-performance luminescent inks for multi-responsive optical patterns (Video S1 and S2, in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 4(a), numeric codes of "92451" are handwritten with the MOFs@SiNPs ink on commercial A4 paper. After complete drying in ambient conditions, the characters are nearly invisible under daylight. When exposed under UV irradiation, no numbers can be recognized due to the strong fluorescence background of the paper. In
sharp contrast, the correct number can be obtained when the UV lamp is turned off. This result can be attributed to the long phosphorescence lifetime of the MOFs@SiNPs under room temperature. As a proof-of-concept application, the feasibility of MOFs@SiNPs as a kind of superior security ink for numbers encryption is further investigated. As illustrated in Fig. 4(b), on commercial black paper, the desired different parts of numbers are drawn using the corresponding materials and a white pattern of "821" is visible under daylight. In addition, wrong information (blue numbers of "34" and orange numbers of "12") is observed upon UV irradiation. In marked contrast, the correct information (number "31") can be obtained by switching off UV irradiation.
Fingerprints featuring the unique ridge skin patterns hold a wealth of information, which have been applied extensively in identity recognition. In our daily life, fingerprint patterns are often applied in documents, diplomas and certificates to make them hard to duplicate and easy to authenticate. Therefore, we further study the feasibility of the MOFs@SiNPs-based ink for fingerprints recognition application. In our experiment, fingers stained with the MOFs@SiNPs ink are pressed on commercial black paper, and three-level details of the fingerprint features could be clearly indicated through observing fluorescence and phosphorescence signals of the MOFs@SiNPs. Specifically, three basic fingerprint patterns (level 1) include whorl (Fig. 5(a)), loop (Fig. 5(b)) and double loop whorls (Fig. S15 in the ESM). Both fluorescent and phosphorescent images display well-resolved ridge flow and pattern structure of the fingerprints. In addition, a number of magnified images of these fingerprints are shown in Fig. 5(c), which clearly illustrate details of the fingerprints (termination (level 2), bridge (level 2), short ridge (level 2), and creases (level 3)) [48, 49]. These detailed characteristics will be useful for discerning personal identifying features and providing prima facie evidence in the identification of partial or damaged fingerprints. Moreover, the precise signal-to-noise ratios of phosphorescence and fluorescence imaging are calculated to be 233.64 and 34, respectively, indicating that the MOFs@SiNPs can effectively increase the imaging sensitivity and signal-to-noise ratio. For information protection and other anti-counterfeiting applications, the fingerprints need to be highly resistant to atmosphere and can be stored for long
time. Therefore, we further demonstrate the stability of these MOFs@SiNPs-based fingerprints exposed to ambient conditions without any specific protection. As shown in Fig. S16 in the ESM, after 30 days of exposure in the atmosphere, these fingerprints maintain stable and bright fluorescence when irradiated by \(365~\mathrm{nm}\) irradiation. When the UV lamp is turned off, the fingerprints still can be easily recognized. The above results clearly demonstrate that the prepared MOFs@SiNPs possess excellent stability.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, a novel kind of luminescent material, i.e., MOFs@SiNPs, has been prepared through a facile microwave-assisted method. Significantly, the MOFs@SiNPs display pH-responsive fluorescence emission (maximum emission wavelength from 442 to \(592~\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to \(\mathrm{pH}\) value from 2 to 13), as well as strong room-temperature phosphorescence (maximum emission at \(\sim505~\mathrm{nm}\)) with long lifetime of \(215~\mathrm{ms}\). Further studies demonstrate that the MOFs@SiNPs could be utilized as high-efficacy security inks for multi-modal anti-counterfeiting in number security and fingerprint fields, showing high promise in the field of data storage, information protection and advanced encryption. We expect that this study may offer exciting opportunities for designing new kinds of fluorescent and phosphorescent Si-based materials, facilitating the promotion of Si-based widespread optical applications. Moreover, our results would provide powerful tools for multimodal advanced anti-counterfeiting and encryption with higher-level information security.
## 4 Experimental section
## 4.1 Synthesis of SiNPs
Fluorescent SiNPs were readily synthesized through microwave-assisted method based on the previous report [50]. In details, trisodium citrate dihydrate \((0.075~\mathrm{g})\) was dissolved by \(8~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)-saturated Milli-Q water in a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) round-bottom flask. Then \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) (3-aminopropyl) trimethoxysilane was added into the flask. The above solution was stirred for \(10~\mathrm{min}\). The mixed
solution was then transferred into a \(30\,\mathrm{mL}\) exclusive vitreous vessel. After \(120\,\mathrm{min}\) microwave reaction at \(160\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the sample was collected. Dialysis \((500\,\mathrm{Da})\) was used to purify the obtained sample. The final products were obtained as a white powder by freeze-drying.
## 4.2 Synthesis of MOFs
A type of \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) -based MOFs (namely \([\mathrm{Zn(TPA)(DMF)}]\) ) featuring room-temperature phosphorescence was synthesized according to a previously reported method [35]. Briefly, 1,4-dicarboxybenzene (TPA, \(166~\mathrm{mg}\) ) and \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF were added to the exclusive vitreous vessel and sonicated for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) . Then, \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((595~\mathrm{mg})\) and water \(\mathrm{[0.5\mL]}\) ) were added to the vessel. After 6-h reaction at \(100\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) , the sample was collected when the temperature cooled to \(<30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) naturally. Finally, the obtained sample was washed with DMF several times.
## 4.3 Synthesis of MOFs@SiNPs
In details, TPA \(\left(166\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and DMF \(\mathrm{[0\mL]}\) ) were added to the vitreous vessel and sonicated for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) . Then, \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((595~\mathrm{mg})\) , SiNPs \(\left(0.0020\ \mathrm{g}\right)\) and water \(\mathrm{[0.5\mL]}\) ) were added to the the vessel. After 6-h reaction at \(100\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) , the sample was collected when the temperature cooled to \(<30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) naturally. The obtained precipitate was washed with DMF.
## 4.4 Anti-counterfeiting application
The desired numbers were printed onto papers and then these numbers were drawn with the corresponding materials (i.e., MOFs@SiNPs, SiNPs and MOFs@SiNPs dispersions in NaOH solution \(\mathrm{(pH\,=\,13)}\) ). The fluorescence and phosphorescence images were captured by a digital camera under \(365~\mathrm{\nm}\) irradiation and UV excitation off, respectively. Typically, a Maestro in vivo optical imaging system (Cambridge Research & instrumentation, Inc.) was utilized for imaging the fingerprints on commercial black paper developed with the MOFs@SiNPs ink. In particular, excitation wavelength: \(365~\mathrm{nm}\) (an additional UV lamp), blue emission window: \(420{-}460\ \mathrm{nm}\) and orange emission window: \(580{-}620\ \mathrm{nm}\) , exposure time: \(1{,}000~\mathrm{ms}\) ; UV excitation off, green emission window: \(490{-}530\ \mathrm{nm}\) , exposure time: \(1{,}000\;\mathrm{ms}\) .
